    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm particularly concerned about the \"acting up\" part during edge cases where the heat soak leads to uneven friction. If the thermal management fails and we see intermittent, unpredictable clamping forces or strange noises during heavy braking, how would you characterize that kind of sudden mechanical instability in the braking system?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This sudden mechanical instability is best characterized as **thermal runaway**, a destructive feedback loop where overheating causes friction coefficients to fluctuate wildly or zero out. As temperatures approach critical thresholds, the brake pads can experience \"fading\" due to chemical changes in the friction material or physical glazing of the rotor surface, leading to unpredictable clamping forces. These conditions often generate high-frequency vibrations (feeling like \"strange noises\") caused by uneven heat expansion or shattering due to thermally induced stress fractures in the rotor. In extreme thermal events, this instability can culminate in total braking failure if the disc becomes warped or the pads lose all traction against the rotor.",
      "source": "target",
