    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Right, so it's more about the material physics failing than something being \"wrong\" with the assembly itself. But in our testing, we've seen these really erratic, almost \"mischievous\" little hiccups in the clamping pressure—things that don't seem to follow the thermal gradient perfectly. Is there any way that microscopic surface inconsistencies or small contaminant particles could cause that kind of erratic, unpredictable behavior during the heat spike?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, microscopic surface inconsistencies and contaminants can absolutely trigger erratic clamping pressure during a heat spike, as periodontal debris or uneven rotor residue creates localized, intermittent contact points that bypass the average thermal gradient. As the heat concentrates at these micro-contacts, the friction coefficient can drop locally (creating \"hiccups\" in pressure) while adjacent areas behave normally, resulting in chaotic vibration patterns rather than a uniform fade. This phenomenon is often exacerbated by carbon-ceramic dust re-deposition on the brake rotor, which acts as an abrasive that alters thermal conductivity and friction behavior unpredictably as temperature rises. Consequently, the compounding effect of these microscopic \"pop-off\" points disrupts the clamping stability, making the brake feel inconsistent even when the broader thermal management system appears functional.",
      "source": "target",
